WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:04.290
Behold, this infinite
mathematical universe...

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:07.862
a dizzying cascade of fractals.

00:00:07.862 --> 00:00:12.731
Arrays of complex patterns filled with both 
satisfying self-similarity

00:00:12.731 --> 00:00:15.293
and surprising, novel features.

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:19.800
All of the structure and the patterns
that you see there really do exist for a reason.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:24.120
There is some sort of innate mathematical
cause behind all of the phenomenon

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:24.953
that you see.

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:26.850
This is the Mandelbrot set.

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:31.650
These trippy images represent a form
of modern algorithmic exploration,

00:00:32.190 --> 00:00:34.350
one made possible by computers.

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:40.403
A handful of mathematicians have devoted
their lives to uncovering this set's secrets.

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:44.400
It plays this very central role
in our understanding of much,

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:48.270
much, more complicated systems.
It is step number one.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:51.960
And we may be on the verge
of finally understanding it.

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:56.550
So what exactly is the Mandelbrot
set and how does it work?

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:02.760
The Mandelbrot set is a perfect example
of how a simple rule can produce

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:04.366
incredible complexity.

00:01:04.366 --> 00:01:05.700
At its core,

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:08.790
the set is generated by
iterating a quadratic equation,

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:12.180
a simple formula whose
highest exponent is two.

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:16.650
To iterate a quadratic equation,
choose a value for the variable,

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:18.540
plug it into the function,

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:22.380
then take the output and feed
it back in again and again.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:29.280
The study of how recursive functions like
these change over time is central to a

00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:32.280
field of math called
Complex Dynamical Systems.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:36.270
This subject came about with the
goal of understanding the real world.

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:40.530
What are the mathematics that underlie
the physical world that we see?

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:44.190
Most important early example
would've been the solar system.

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:48.810
So dynamical systems that you have some
space where you have some rule that's

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:50.820
assigning as time moves on,

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:53.340
how that space evolves and
how things move and change.

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:56.790
In the early 20th century, mathematicians,

00:01:56.790 --> 00:02:01.200
Pierre Fatou and Gaston Julia set
the stage for the discovery of the

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.145
Mandelbrot set with their
exploration of dynamical systems.

00:02:05.145 --> 00:02:09.930
To investigate these dynamical systems,
mathematicians study intricate shapes.

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:11.970
today known as Julia Sets,

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:16.890
Julis sets are produced by iterating
a function of complex numbers.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.910
A complex number is defined
as the sum of two components,

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.550
a real part and an imaginary part.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.840
Each complex number can be
visualized as a point in a 2D plane.

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:31.680
The real part is a number
found on the number line.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:36.360
The imaginary part is a multiple
of the square root of negative one,

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:38.662
which mathematicians write
as 'i'.

00:02:38.662 --> 00:02:40.230
Despite the name,

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:44.340
imaginary numbers play a vital role
in solving real world problems.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:48.810
To construct a Julia set, start
with a simple quadratic equation.

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:51.960
F of Z equals Z squared plus C.

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:55.920
Choose a value for C like
negative one for example.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:59.272
Then consider what happens when you
iterate this equation

00:02:59.272 --> 00:03:01.665
for every possible starting value.

00:03:01.665 --> 00:03:03.161
You repeatedly apply the function

00:03:03.161 --> 00:03:05.140
to the sequence of numbers
that you're generating,

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:08.381
and you ask whether or not that sequence
is going off to infinity

00:03:08.381 --> 00:03:10.023
or whether it stays bounded.

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:14.440
For some initial values, your
equation speeds off to infinity

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:15.758
when iterated like this.

00:03:15.758 --> 00:03:18.460
These values are not in a Julia set.

00:03:19.570 --> 00:03:22.210
When you start iterating
from other initial values,

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:25.600
you might instead get a sequence
of outputs that stay bounded.

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:30.880
When something comes back to itself,
often we call that recurrent behavior,

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:32.890
and that's where the complexity arises.

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:37.690
The boundary between points that stay
bounded and those that don't, is a Julia Set.

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:41.860
You can fill it in by
including all the bounded values.

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:46.470
Different quadratic equations generate
a wide range of filled Julia sets.

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:49.677
From basic blobs to intricate twisting tendrils.

00:03:49.677 --> 00:03:54.220
Filled Juliia sets can
be divided into two categories.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:58.870
Sets where you can draw a line from one
point to any other without lifting your

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:00.527
pen are connected.

00:04:00.527 --> 00:04:03.869
Sets where points look like scattered pieces of dust

00:04:03.869 --> 00:04:04.990
are disconnected.

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:11.140
The first rough plot of the Mandelbrot
set appeared in a 1978 paper by the

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:14.470
mathematicians Robert
Brooks and J. Peter Matelski.

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:18.550
Soon after Benoit Mandelbrot,
a researcher at IBM,

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:22.270
who had access to more computing
power, also discovered the set.

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:24.910
This led to further explorations.

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:27.940
These early computer graphics were crude,

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:31.750
but the patterns revealed the presence
of something far more complex.

00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:34.330
Even with fuzzy,

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:37.810
poorly made pictures in the Eighties,

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:42.663
they were able to really glean a lot of
interesting insight into what's going on.

00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:46.960
Mandelbrot went on to popularize
his now eponymous set to the world and

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:49.410
became known as the
father of fractals.

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:55.593
Today, mathematicians can use computers to
explore the Mandelbrot set in far greater detail.

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:59.860
As soon as you have images
just opens up this whole world.

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:03.130
The Mandelbrot set is
drawn in the complex plane.

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:07.682
It's constructed by iterating the same
quadratic equations used to produce

00:05:07.682 --> 00:05:08.182
 

00:05:08.182 --> 00:05:10.960
Julia sets. But here things are flipped around.

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:15.370
Instead of iterating all values
of Z for a fixed value of C,

00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:20.080
we fix the starting value of
the iteration at zero and vary C.

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:21.640
Values of C,

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:27.464
where iterations of Z squared plus C
stay bounded are inside the Mandelbrot set.

00:05:27.464 --> 00:05:29.990
While those that go to infinity are not.

00:05:29.990 --> 00:05:35.140
Here, we can select points inside the Mandelbrot
set to reveal their corresponding

00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:36.325
filled Julia sets.

00:05:36.325 --> 00:05:38.950
The Mandelbrot set acts like an atlas,

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:41.620
cataloging the different
kinds of Julia sets.

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:47.170
Values of C inside the Mandelbrot set
are associated with connected Julia sets.

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:51.250
While values outside the set
correspond with disconnected dust.

00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:55.900
The Mandelbrot set has
many intriguing features,

00:05:56.830 --> 00:06:00.350
but its biggest mysteries lie
in its complex fractal boundary.

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:05.750
Zooming in to different boundary regions
reveals some astounding features.

00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:08.330
A valley of seahorses,

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:14.270
parades of elephants and miniature
versions of the set itself.

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:19.280
So we can sort of keep finding nests, a
sequence of smaller and smaller Mandelbrot sets

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:21.290
sets, all one inside of the next.

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:25.190
Here at the boundary, mathematicians
are probing for answers.

00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:29.150
As you look at it, it sort of seems
like one blob, but if you really ask,

00:06:29.570 --> 00:06:31.880
is it all connected? Or maybe
if I zoom in far enough,

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:33.260
there's some separating piece.

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:36.703
This question is central to a 40-year-old
problem

00:06:36.703 --> 00:06:39.411
called the Mandelbrot Locally Connected Conjecture

00:06:39.411 --> 00:06:40.550
or MLC.

00:06:41.630 --> 00:06:43.340
If the set is locally connected,

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:48.550
that means that no matter what point you
choose to examine or how far you zoom in,

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:52.040
the area will always look
like one nice connected section.

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:55.490
For example, if we
closely examine a circle,

00:06:55.580 --> 00:06:58.430
we can see that it's locally
connected at every point,

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:01.910
but take a fine-toothed comb and zoom in.

00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:05.800
While the comb is one connected shape,

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:07.310
at close range, it can look disconnected.

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:11.900
If mathematicians can
prove that MLC is true,

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:16.220
then a complete understanding of the
Mandelbrot set would be within reach.

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:20.240
This local connectivity question, if
we can answer it, if it can be solved,

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:24.080
this will give us almost complete control
and understanding of what's going on.

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:27.080
The hope is that once we completely
understand quadratic polynomials

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:31.320
we can go on to looking at more complicated dynamical
systems

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:32.660
and be able to describe them more and more.

00:07:33.410 --> 00:07:37.280
But a full understanding of the
Mandelbrot set wouldn't put an end to our

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:40.280
collective fascination
with this famous fractal.

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:43.880
Hard to imagine that it wouldn't
be interesting to people,

00:07:44.210 --> 00:07:46.370
even if we fully understood it,

00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:50.030
because it is so complicated
and so rich and so beautiful.

